{:up ["CREATE TABLE recipe_events (
         id           INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
         user_id      INTEGER REFERENCES users(id),
         recipe_id    INTEGER NOT NULL,
         recipe_title TEXT NOT NULL,
         kind         TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (kind IN ('created','modified','deleted','proposed')),
         version      INTEGER,
         proposal_id  INTEGER,
         created_at   DATETIME DEFAULT (datetime('now')),
         seen         INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (seen IN (0,1))
       )"
      "CREATE INDEX idx_recipe_events_user_seen ON recipe_events (user_id, seen, id)"]
 ;; The owner's **inbox**: one row per change **an agent** made to a Recipe, in the
 ;; order the changes happened, unseen until he says otherwise. `et.cb.db.event`
 ;; owns the table and `et.cb.server.inbox-handler` serves it.
 ;;
 ;; **It is not a change log.** He was asked whether his own UI edits belong in it
 ;; and said no: this is the record of what the agents did to his shelf while he
 ;; was not looking, which is also what makes going through it oldest-first worth
 ;; doing. So a row exists here exactly when the write it records was stamped
 ;; `source = 'machine'` — the same fact, off the same token claim, and not a
 ;; second guess about who was calling.
 ;;
 ;; **A table and not a derivation, which is the other decision here.** The queue
 ;; could look derivable from `recipe_history` plus a seen-marker, and it is not: a
 ;; history row's `created_at` takes sqlite's `datetime('now')` at the moment
 ;; `archive!` inserts it, which is when the *next* save displaced that version —
 ;; `ui.views.diff/when-label` spells this out where a reader meets it. So nothing
 ;; in the schema before 009 could answer "when was v2 written", and a queue
 ;; ordered on that column would have been ordered on when each version *stopped*
 ;; being current: silently wrong rather than visibly broken. An event is recorded
 ;; as the thing happens, which is also the honest home for that missing timestamp.
 ;;
 ;; **The queue is `id` ascending, not `created_at` ascending.** `created_at` is
 ;; second-resolution here as everywhere in this schema, and two events landing in
 ;; one second is the normal case rather than a corner — a create plus the
 ;; proposal an agent files a moment later. `id` is the append order, and the
 ;; append order is what was asked for: newer items go bottomwards. It is the
 ;; house rule the shelf's own ranking already follows: a view's ordering gets a
 ;; column of its own rather than borrowing another view's.
 ;;
 ;; **No `source` column, because every event here is a machine's.** An event's
 ;; source has exactly one possible value, and a column that can only ever hold one
 ;; answer answers nothing — the argument migration 007 already made about
 ;; `category_type`, and the one 010 makes again about `recipe_proposals`. Do not
 ;; add one "for symmetry" with `recipes.source`: that column exists because a
 ;; version really can have come from three places (see 005), and this cannot.
 ;;
 ;; **`proposal_id` and the `'proposed'` kind are declared here although nothing
 ;; writes either until 010.** Widening a SQLite `CHECK` means rebuilding the
 ;; table; a nullable integer column costs nothing at all. So this is declared for
 ;; the whole design it belongs to rather than for half of it, and 010 stays a
 ;; pure addition — the alternative would have been a table rebuild to add one
 ;; word to a constraint.
 ;;
 ;; **`recipe_title` is a snapshot, and `recipe_id` is deliberately not a foreign
 ;; key with a cascade.** Events outlive their Recipe: `db.recipe/delete-recipe`
 ;; deletes a Recipe's history and its Scope associations — those are parts of a
 ;; Recipe — and leaves its events alone, because an event is the record that
 ;; something happened and the something did happen. Deleting them would let an
 ;; agent create a Recipe and delete it again and erase the inbox's record of
 ;; both, which is a hole in the one thing the inbox promises. The title snapshot
 ;; is what keeps such an orphaned event readable. `recipes` is `INTEGER PRIMARY
 ;; KEY AUTOINCREMENT`, which in SQLite never reuses an id, so a dangling
 ;; `recipe_id` can never come to name a different Recipe.
 ;;
 ;; **`seen` is a bare 0/1 flag**, following tracker's `messages.done` — the same
 ;; concept in the same suite. No `seen_at`: the queue is ordered by `id`, nothing
 ;; would read such a stamp, and a column nothing reads is one that goes
 ;; unmaintained. The `CHECK` is what keeps it a flag rather than a small integer
 ;; somebody starts counting with.
 ;;
 ;; `user_id` is nullable for the reason every other table's is: dev's owner has
 ;; no `users` row, and `db/user-id-where-clause` reads a missing id as
 ;; `user_id IS NULL`, which is a real owner in this schema. Every read of this
 ;; table goes through that function and never through `= user-id`.
 ;;
 ;; The index is `(user_id, seen, id)`, which is exactly the one query this table
 ;; exists for: one owner's unseen events in append order.
 :down ["DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_recipe_events_user_seen"
        "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS recipe_events"]}
